1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bacalla_de_Luna
2 CONT
2 CONT Bacalla, Briocalla, Bachalla, Bacahalla o Bacalha de Luna (? - 1115 o 1120) fue un caballero aragonés de origen navarro, conquistador de la villa de Luna de donde tomó el apellido y fue el primero del Linaje de los Luna.
2 CONT
2 CONT Se le atribuye un casamiento con Sancha Sánchez, supuestamente una hija ilegítima del rey Sancho I Ramírez de Aragón, con la que habría tenido tres hijos:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Lope Ferrench I de Luna, II señor de Luna y cabeza del linaje de los Ferrench de Luna, presente en la batalla de Alcoraz el 15 de noviembre de 1096.
2 CONT # Gómez I de Luna, presente en la batalla de Alcoraz el 15 de noviembre de 1096 y muerto en el batalla de Fraga en septiembre de 1134.
2 CONT # Íñigo Ferrench de Luna, colaborador de Alfonso I de Aragón.
2 CONT
2 CONT La falta de atestiguación en fuentes del periodo de varios de estos familiares y la existencia de teorías alternativas que hacen descender a la casa de Luna del conde Sancho Ramírez (hermano del rey homónimo)[5]​ hacen que esta ge
2 CONC nealogía sea también dudosa en la historiografía actual.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Google translate 
2 CONT
2 CONT Bacalla , Briocalla , Bachalla , Bacahalla or Bacalha de Luna (? - 1115 or 1120) was an Aragonese knight of Navarrese origin , conqueror of the town of Luna from where he took his surname and was the first of the Luna lineage .
2 CONT
2 CONT He appears cited in ancient sources as the son of a Lope Ferrench and his wife Ximena Martínez, daughter of Martín Gómez and his wife. In these sources, he is credited with having participated in the reconquest of Luna , after whi
2 CONC ch he would have been named its first lieutenant and, as stated in the Chronicle of San Juan de la Peña , to have fought alongside his sons Lope Ferrench I de Luna and Gómez I de Luna in the Battle of Alcoraz on November 15, 1096.
2 CONC  [ 1 ]​ These data are however doubtful for modern historiography, since from the works of Antonio Ubieto Arteta it has been possible to verify that most of the characters mentioned do not coincide with the documentation of the pe
2 CONC riod, being instead mentioned for the first time in works centuries after the events. [ 2 ]
2 CONT
2 CONT The primary sources preserved mention instead that after the conquest of Luna by Sancho Ramírez , the town was placed under the royal merino Banzo Azcones, whom some identify as the historical Bacalla. [ 3 ] In that case he would 
2 CONC have remained in Luna during the reign of Sancho Ramírez and his sons Pedro and Alfonso , receiving royal rewards and donations for his role in the organization of the town and in the construction of defenses in that sector of the
2 CONC  border with the Muslims. [ 4 ]
2 CONT
2 CONT He is credited with marrying Sancha Sánchez, supposedly an illegitimate daughter of King Sancho I Ramírez of Aragon , with whom he would have had three children:
2 CONT # Lope Ferrench I de Luna , second Lord of Luna and head of the Ferrench de Luna lineage , present at the Battle of Alcoraz on 15 November 1096.
2 CONT # Gómez I de Luna , present at the Battle of Alcoraz on November 15, 1096 and died at the Battle of Fraga in September 1134.
2 CONT # Íñigo Ferrench de Luna, collaborator of Alfonso I of Aragon .
2 CONT The lack of attestation in sources from the period of several of these relatives and the existence of alternative theories that make the House of Luna descend from Count Sancho Ramírez (brother of the king of the same name) [ 5 ] 
2 CONC make this genealogy also doubtful in current historiography.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ARAGONESE%20NOBILITY.htm#PedroLunaMElsaEjerica
2 CONT
2 CONT C.      LUNA
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Cariñena’s late 14th century (with continuations) Nobiliario de Aragón provides a reconstruction of the family of Lope Ferrench [I] de Luna who is shown below, also showing a suggested relationship with Pedro Martínez de Luna “e
2 CONC l Viejo” who is shown further below in the present section[997].  The accuracy of this information is not known and no attempt has been made to incorporate it into the following reconstruction.  On the other hand, Cariñena, in ano
2 CONC ther section entitled “Luna escaquada. Señores de Luna, Erla y Segorbe”, includes more detailed information about the “Ferrench de Luna” family, some of which has been included as noted below (although that information is not prob
2 CONC lem free as will be seen)[998].'' 
2 CONT
2 CONT *1.         BOCALLA .  Zurita records that “Luna” was populated during the reign of King Sancho I who granted “el señorío de Luna a D. Bachalla segun parece en memorias antiguas”, dated to 1091 from the context, and that the Luna 
2 CONC families called “Ferrenches y Artales” descended from him[999].  Zurita names “Don Ferriz de Liçana, D. Bachalla, Don Garcia de Atrofillo, Don Lope Ferrench de Luna y Don Gomez de Luna” among those who fought at Zaragoza with King
2 CONC  Pedro I in 1096[1000]. 
2 CONT **2.         LÓPEZ de Luna .  Palacios Sánchez names “López de Luna” as the third of the three sons of “Don Bocalla de Luna”, adding that he was ancestor of “los López de Luna de Aragón”[1001]. 
2 CONT **3.         LOPE Ferrench [I] de Luna (-killed in battle Huesca 1136).  Zurita names “Don Ferriz de Liçana, D. Bachalla, Don Garcia de Atrofillo, Don Lope Ferrench de Luna y Don Gomez de Luna” among those who fought at Zaragoza w
2 CONC ith King Pedro I in 1096[1002].  Palacios Sánchez names “Don Lope Ferrench de Luna” as the oldest of the three sons of “Don Bocalla de Luna”, adding that he was ancestor of “los señores y condes de Luna de Aragón”[1003].  Zurita n
2 CONC ames “Lope Ferrench de Luna, Ruy Ximeney de Luna, Pedro Martinez de Luna, Fernando y Gomez de Luna…” among the nobles of Ramiro King of Aragon who were killed at Huesca in 1136[1004]. 
2 CONT ***4.         PEDRO López de Luna (-after 1174).  Cariñena records “Don Pedro López de Luna…como sucesor y hijo de don Lope Ferrench” [the accuracy of the family relationship is unknown], noting that he was named in documents date
2 CONC d 1156 and 1174[1005].  m ---.  The name of Pedro’s wife is not known.  Pedro & his wife had one child: 
2 CONT ****a)         LOPE Ferrench [II] de Luna .  Cariñena records a charter “del rey don Alonsso” dated 1178 which names “Lop Ferrench, filio Petri Lupi de Luna”, as well as two documents dated 1190 and 1192, and an undated document o
2 CONC f Pedro I King of Aragon, which name him[1006]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Casa_de_Luna
2 CONT
2 CONT La casa de Luna es una casa nobiliaria española, originaria del reino de Pamplona y establecida en el Reino de Aragón.  ….
2 CONT
2 CONT ….Lope Ferrench y Jimena, según algunos autores,[4]​ tuvieron por hijo a Bacahalla o Bacalla de Luna.[b]​Bacalla, cuyo nombre realmente fue Banzo Azcón, fue el primer freire que tuvo a su mando la encomienda de Luna de la Orden de
2 CONC l Temple.[6]​ Fue un ricohombre de Aragón que se distinguió en la toma de la villa de Luna —actual provincia de Zaragoza— en 1092, en tiempos del rey Sancho I de Aragón y V de Pamplona y le fue encomendada la tenencia de la plaza 
2 CONC poco después de la repoblación,[7]​ cuando el rey aragonés le donó en 1093 el terreno donde se construyó la fortaleza en Luna,[6]​ que se transformaría en señorío hereditario,[1]​ aunque esto no ocurrió hasta 1343 ya que hasta ese
2 CONC  año la villa fue realengo.[8]​[c]​
2 CONT
2 CONT Los tres hijos de Bacalla con su mujer Sancha Sánchez, supuestamente una hija ilegítima del rey Sancho Ramírez de Aragón, fueron Lope Ferrench de Luna (f. Huesca, 1136), II señor de Luna, cabeza del linaje de los Ferrench de Luna 
2 CONC y posteriores condes de Luna, Gome de Luna (f. ib., 1134) quien con su hermano mayor y su padre se hallaron en la batalla de Alcoraz en 1096, e Íñigo de Luna.[1]​
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Google translate 
2 CONT
2 CONT The House of Luna is a Spanish noble house, originating in the Kingdom of Pamplona and established in the Kingdom of Aragon. ….
2 CONT
2 CONT ….Lope Ferrench and Jimena, according to some authors,[4]​ had as a son Bacahalla or Bacalla de Luna.[b]​ Bacalla, whose real name was Banzo Azcón, was the first friar who had command of the Luna commandery of the Order of the Tem
2 CONC ple.[6]​ He was a nobleman of Aragon who distinguished himself in the capture of the town of Luna —current province of Zaragoza— in 1092, during the time of King Sancho I of Aragon and V of Pamplona and was entrusted with the tenu
2 CONC re of the square shortly after the repopulation,[7]​ when the Aragonese king donated him in 1093 the land where the fortress was built in Luna,[6]​ which would become a hereditary lordship,[1]​ although this did not occur until 13
2 CONC 43 since until that year the town was royal.[8]​ [c]​
2 CONT
2 CONT Bacalla's three children with his wife Sancha Sánchez, supposedly an illegitimate daughter of King Sancho Ramírez of Aragon, were Lope Ferrench de Luna (d. Huesca, 1136), 2nd Lord of Luna, head of the lineage of the Ferrench de Lu
2 CONC na and later Counts of Luna, Gome de Luna (d. ib., 1134) who with his elder brother and father were present at the Battle of Alcoraz in 1096, and Íñigo de Luna.[1]
2 CONT
