0 @N6000000003637348918@ NOTE Conde de Trastamara, Monterroso y Trava. Ayo del Emperador Alfonso VII. y de María Ansúrez, señora de Valladolid. Él y su esposa dieron fuero a su villa de Tardajas, a tres leguas de Burgos. También llamado Pedro Fruelas de Trava 
1 CONC y Pedro Fernández de Trava. Doña Urraca le hace donación del desaparecido castillo de Leiro en Abegondo. Falleció en el año de 1126. Estuvo casado en primeras con Doña Urraca Froilaz condesa propietaria de Aranga y en segundas con
1 CONC  Doña Mayor de Urgel o Mayor Gutronda Rodríguez, hija del conde Armengol y de su mujer Doña Mayor Pérez Azures, señora de Valladolid, nieta paterna del conde Armengol de Gerp y de su mujer Doña Luciana.
0 @N6000000000437131523@ NOTE Name Prefix:<NPFX> Queen Name Suffix:<NSFX> Of Castile
0 @N6000000001353965301@ NOTE Urraca (b. 1077-81--d. March 8, 1126, Saldana, Castile [Spain]), queen of Leon and Castile from 1109 to 1126, daughter of Alfonso VI.
1 CONT Urraca became her father's heiress when her brother, Sancho, was killed at Uclés (1108). She was the widow of Count Raymond of Burgundy, bywhom she had had one son, Alfonso Ramírez (born 1104), the future Alfonso VII. To counterba
1 CONC lance--it was hoped--the dangers of a female succession during the Almoravid crisis, Urraca's marriage to her second cousin, Alfonso I of Aragon, was arranged (1109). This marriage, instead of producing political stability in Urra
1 CONC ca's kingdom, led to years of anarchy. Urraca and her husband, according to the marriage settlement, became corulers in each other's lands, and Alfonso thereupon put Aragonese garrisons into many Leonese and Castilian cities. The 
1 CONC notion of an Aragonese-Castilian political union was, however, premature, andalthough Urraca's municipalities tended to accept the Aragonese king,the magnates were hostile. Civil war broke out and continued for years, many support
1 CONC ing the claims of the child Alfonso Ramírez to the throne. Matters were further complicated by the temperamental incompatibility of Urraca and her husband, who soon quarreled. Urraca was tenacious of her right as proprietary queen
1 CONC  and had not learnt chastity in the polygamous household of her father. Husband and wife quarrelled with the brutality of the age, and came to open war. Alphonso had the support of one section of the nobles who found their account
1 CONC  in the confusion. Being a much better soldier than any of his opponents he gainedvictories at Sepúlveda and Fuente de la Culebra, but his only trustworthy supporters were his Aragonese who were not numerous enough to keep down Ca
1 CONC stile and Leon. Pope Paschal II, moreover, declared their marriage canonically invalid. They finally separated in 1114, though theAragonese king continued for some years thereafter to keep his garrisons in Castile and to use the r
1 CONC oyal title.
1 CONT Struggles also continued between nobles and municipalities, between rival bands of magnates, between the archbishops of Santiago and Toledo, and between the former, the bishop Diego Gelmírez, and Urraca herself. Alfonso Ramírez wa
1 CONC s crowned by Gelmírez in 1111, and his reign in Galicia began effectively -- despite Urraca's intermittent but active opposition--in 1116. Urraca's death in 1126 ended a disastrous episodein the medieval political history of Chris
1 CONC tian Spain
