0 @N6000000002187750703@ NOTE Alfonso VII of León
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Alfonso VII (1 March 1105 – 21 August 1157), called the Emperor, became the King of Galicia in 1111 and King of León and Castile in 1126. He was crowned Emperor of Spain in 1135. He was the son of Urraca of Castile and Raymond of 
1 CONC Burgundy; the first of the House of Burgundy to rule in Spain.
1 CONT
1 CONT Alfonso was a dignified and somewhat enigmatic figure. His rule was characterised by the renewed supremacy of the western kingdoms of Christian Spain over the eastern (Navarre and Aragón) after the reign of Alfonso the Battler. He
1 CONC  also sought to make the imperial title meaningful in practice, though his attempts to rule over both Christian and Moslem populations was even less successful. His hegemonic intentions never saw fruition, however. During his tenu
1 CONC re, Portugal became de facto independent, in 1128, and was recognized as de jure independent, in 1143. He was a patron of poets, including, probably, the troubadour Marcabru.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Succession to three kingdoms 
1 CONT 2 Territorial aspirations 
1 CONT 3 Imperial rule 
1 CONT 4 Reconquista 
1 CONT 5 Legacy 
1 CONT 6 Family 
1 CONT 7 Sources 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Succession to three kingdoms
1 CONT In 1111, Diego Gelmírez, Bishop of Compostela, and the count of Traba crowned Alfonso King of Galicia in the cathedral of Santiago de Compostela. He was but a child at the time, but his mother had already (1109) succeeded to the u
1 CONC nited throne of León-Castile-Galicia and desired to assure her son's prospects and groom him for his eventual succession.
1 CONT
1 CONT On 10 March 1126, after the death of his mother, he was crowned in León and immediately began the recovery of the Kingdom of Castile, which was then under the domination of Alfonso the Battler. By the Peace of Támara of 1127, the 
1 CONC Battler recognised Alfonso VII of Castile. The territory in the far east of his dominion, however, had gained much independence during the rule of his mother and experienced many rebellions. After his recognition in Castile, Alfon
1 CONC so fought to curb the autonomy of the local barons.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Territorial aspirations
1 CONT When Alfonso the Battler, King of Navarre and Aragón, died without descendants in 1134, he willed his kingdom to the military orders. The aristocracy of both kingdoms did not accept this and García VI was elected in Navarre while 
1 CONC Alfonso pretended to the throne of Aragón. The nobles chose another candidate in the dead king's brother, Ramiro II. Alfonso responded by occupying La Rioja, conquering Zaragoza, and governing both realms in unison. From this poin
1 CONC t, the arms of Zaragoza began to appear in those of León.
1 CONT
1 CONT In several skirmished, he defeated the joint Navarro-Aragonese army and put the kingdoms to vassalage. He had the strong support of the lords north of the Pyrenees, who held lands as far as the River Rhône. In the end, however, th
1 CONC e combined forces of the Navarre and Aragón were too much for his control.
1 CONT
1 CONT At this time, he helped Ramon Berenguer III, Count of Barcelona, in his wars with the other Catalan counties to unite the old Marca Hispanica.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Imperial rule
1 CONT A vague tradition had always assigned the title of emperor to the sovereign who held León. Sancho the Great considered the city the imperiale culmen and minted coins with the inscription Imperator totius Hispaniae after being crow
1 CONC ned in it. Such a sovereign was considered the most direct representative of the Visigothic kings, who had been themselves the representatives of the Roman Empire. But though appearing in charters, and claimed by Alfonso VI of Cas
1 CONC tile and Alfonso the Battler, the title had been little more than a flourish of rhetoric.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1135, Alfonso was crowned "Emperor of All the Spains" in the Cathedral of León. By this, he probably wished to assert his authority over the entire peninsula and his absolute leadership of the Reconquista. He appears to have st
1 CONC riven for the formation of a national unity which Spain had never possessed since the fall of the Visigothic kingdom. The elements he had to deal with could not be welded together. The weakness of Aragón enabled him to make his su
1 CONC periority effective, although Afonso I of Portugal never recognised him as liege, thereby affirming Portugal's independence. In 1143, he himself recognised this status quo and consented to the marriage of Petronila of Aragón with 
1 CONC Ramon Berenguer IV, a union which combined Aragón and Catalonia into the Crown of Aragón.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Reconquista
1 CONT Alfonso was a pious prince. He introduced the Cistercians to Spain by founding a monastery at Fitero. He adopted a militant attitude towards the Moors of al-Andalus, especially the Almoravids.
1 CONT
1 CONT From 1139, Alfonso led a series of expeditions subjugating the Almoravids. He took the fortress of Oreja near Toledo and, as the Chronica Adefonsis Imperatoris tells it:
1 CONT
1 CONT “ . . . early in the morning the castle was surrendered and the towers were filled with Christian knights, and the royal standards were raised above a high tower. Those who held the standards shouted out loud and proclaimed "Long 
1 CONC live Alfonso, emperor of León and Toledo!" ” 
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1146, the Almohads invaded and he was forced to refortify his southern frontier and come to an agreement with the Almoravid Ibn Ganiya for their mutual defence.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1144, Alfonso advanced as far as Córdoba. When Pope Eugene III preached the Second Crusade, Alfonso VII, with García of Navarre and Ramon Berenguer, led a mixed army of Catalans and Franks, with a Genoese-Pisans navy, in a crus
1 CONC ade against the rich port city of Almería, which was occupied in October 1147. It was Castile's first Mediterranean seaport.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1151, Alfonso signed the Treaty of Tudilén with Raymond Berengar IV of Barcelona. The treaty defined the zones of conquest in Andalusia in order to prevent the two rulers from coming into conflict.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1157, Almería entered into Almohad possession. Alfonso was returining from an expedition against them when he died in pass of Muradel in the Sierra Morena, possibly at Viso del Marqués (Ciudad Real).
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Legacy
1 CONT Alfonso was at once a patron of the church and a protector, though not a supporter of, the Moslems, who were a minority of his subjects. His reign ended in an unsuccessful campaign against the rising power of the Almohads. Though 
1 CONC he was not actually defeated, his death in the pass, while on his way back to Toledo, occurred in circumstances which showed that no man could be what he claimed to be — "king of the men of the two religions."
1 CONT
1 CONT Furthermore, by dividing his realm between his son, he ensured that Christendom would not present the new Almohad threat with a united front.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family
1 CONT In November 1128, he married Berenguela, daughter of Ramon Berenguer III. She died in 1149. Their children were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Sancho III of Castile (1134-1158) 
1 CONT Ferdinand II of León (1137-1188) 
1 CONT Sancha (1137-1179), married Sancho VI of Navarre 
1 CONT Constance (1141-1160), married Louis VII of France 
1 CONT In 1152, Alfonso married Richeza of Poland, the daughter of Ladislaus II the Exile. They had a daughter, Sancha (1155-1208), the wife of Alfonso II of Aragón.
1 CONT
1 CONT By his mistress, an Asturian noblewoman named Guntroda, he had an illegitimate daughter, Urraca, who married García VI of Navarre.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT Arnaldo, Bishop of Astorga, wrote an account of Alfonso VII's life and reign known as the Chronica Adefonsi Imperatoris.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Urraca King of Galicia
1 CONT 1111 – 1157 Succeeded by
1 CONT Ferdinand II 
1 CONT King of León
1 CONT 1126 – 1157 
1 CONT King of Castile
1 CONT 1127 – 1157 Succeeded by
1 CONT Sancho III 
1 CONT Vacant 
1 CONT Title last held by
1 CONT Alfonso I Emperor of Spain
1 CONT 1135 – 1157 Succeeded by
1 CONT None 
