0 @N6000000000337154043@ NOTE (Research):The Columbia Encyclopedia, Sixth Edition.  2001.   Sancho I, king of Portugal    c.1154-1211, king of Portugal (1185-1211), son and successor of Alfonso I. He was associated in his father's government from c.1170. Sanch
1 CONC o undertook to restore and repeople the lands devastated in the wars against the Moors; thus he became known as Sancho o Povoador [Sancho the settler or populator]. Early successes against the Moors in Algarve were canceled by new
1 CONC  Moorish attacks. He was succeeded by his son Alfonso II.   Encyclopedia.com  Sancho I Sancho I, c.1154-1211, king of Portugal (1185-1211), son and successor of Alfonso I. He was associated in his father's government from c.1170. 
1 CONC Sancho undertook to restore and repeople the lands devastated in the wars against the Moors; thus he became known as Sancho o Povoador [Sancho the settler or populator]. Early successes against the Moors in Algarve were canceled b
1 CONC y new Moorish attacks. He was succeeded by his son Alfonso II.
0 @N6000000002187755063@ NOTE Sancho I of Portugal
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Sancho I of Portugal (pron. IPA /'s?~?u/), Sanctius I in English, nicknamed the Populator (Port. o Povoador), second king of Portugal, was born on November 11, 1154 in Coimbra and died on March 26, 1212 in the same city. He was th
1 CONC e third but only surviving son of Afonso I Henriques of Portugal by his wife, Mafalda of Savoy. Sancho succeeded his father in 1185. He used the title King of Algarve and/or Silves between 1189 and 1191
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1170, Sancho was knighted by his father, King Afonso I, and from that time he became his second in command, both administratively and military. At this time, the independence of Portugal (declared in 1139) was not firmly establ
1 CONC ished. The kings of León and Castile were trying to annex the country and the Catholic church was late in giving its blessing and approval. Due to this Afonso I had to search for allies within the Iberian Peninsula. Portugal made 
1 CONC an alliance with the kingdom of Aragon and together they fought Castile and León. To secure the agreement, Prince Sancho of Portugal married, in 1174, princess Dulce Berenguer, younger sister of king Alfonso II of Aragon. Aragon w
1 CONC as thus the first Iberian kingdom to recognize the independence of Portugal.
1 CONT
1 CONT With the death of Afonso I in 1185, Sancho I became the second king of Portugal. Coimbra was the centre of his kingdom; Sancho terminated the exhausting and generally pointless wars against his neighbours for control of the Galici
1 CONC an borderlands. Instead, he turned all his attentions to the South, against the Moorish communities that still thrived. With Crusader help he took Silves in 1191. Silves was an important city of the South, an administrative and co
1 CONC mmercial town with population around 20,000 people. Sancho ordered the fortification of the city and built a castle that is today an important monument of Portuguese heritage. However, military attention soon had to be turned agai
1 CONC n to the North, where León and Castile threatened again the Portuguese borders. Silves was again lost to the Moors.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sancho I founded the city of Guarda in 1199.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sancho I dedicated much of his reign to political and administrative organization of the new kingdom. He accumulated a national treasure, supported new industries and the middle class of merchants. Moreover, he created several new
1 CONC  towns and villages and took great care in populating remote areas in the northern Christian regions of Portugal, notably with Flemings and Burgundians – hence the nickname the Populator. The king was also known for his love of kn
1 CONC owledge and literature. Sancho I wrote several books of poems and used the royal treasure to send Portuguese boys to study in European Universities.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Ancestors
1 CONT Sancho's ancestors in three generations Sancho I of Portugal Father:
1 CONT Afonso I of Portugal Father's father:
1 CONT Henry, Count of Portugal Father's father's father:
1 CONT Henry, Duke of Burgundy 
1 CONT Father's father's mother:
1 CONT Sybil (Beatriz) of Barcelona 
1 CONT Father's mother:
1 CONT Theresa, Countess of Portugal Father's mother's father:
1 CONT Alfonso VI of Castile 
1 CONT Father's mother's mother:
1 CONT Jimena Muñoz 
1 CONT Mother:
1 CONT Maud of Savoy Mother's father:
1 CONT Amadeus III of Savoy Mother's father's father:
1 CONT Humbert II of Savoy 
1 CONT Mother's father's mother:
1 CONT Gisela of Burgundy 
1 CONT Mother's mother:
1 CONT Mahaut of Albon Mother's mother's father:
1 CONT Guy II, Count of Albon 
1 CONT Mother's mother's mother:
1 CONT Inés of Barcelona 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Marriage and descendants
1 CONT Sancho married Dulce Berenguer, daughter of Raymond Berengar IV, Count of Barcelona, and Petronilla, Queen of Aragon.
1 CONT
1 CONT Name Birth Death Notes 
1 CONT By Dulce Berenguer (1152-1198; married in 1175) 
1 CONT Theresa 1181 1250 Married to King Alfonso IX of Castile 
1 CONT Raymond c. 1180 1189   
1 CONT Sancha a. 1182 March 13, 1229 Abbess of Lorvão 
