1 CONT Children include
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Infante Miguel of Castile and Portugal
1 CONT
1 CONT Afonso II (Portuguese pronounced [?'fõsu]; English Alphonzo), or Affonso (Archaic Portuguese), Alfonso or Alphonso (Portuguese-Galician) or Alphonsus (Latin version), nicknamed "the Fat" (Portuguese o Gordo), third king of Portuga
1 CONC l, was born in Coimbra on April 23, 1185 and died on March 25, 1223 in the same city. He was the second but eldest surviving son of Sancho I of Portugal by his wife, Dulce Berenguer of Barcelona, Infanta of Aragon. Afonso succeede
1 CONC d his father in 1212.
1 CONT
1 CONT As a king, Afonso II set a different approach of government. Hitherto, his father Sancho I and his grandfather Afonso I, were mostly concerned with military issues either against the neighbouring Kingdom of Castile or against the 
1 CONC Moorish lands in the south. Afonso did not pursue territory enlargement policies and managed to insure peace with Castile during his reign. Despite this, some towns, like Alcácer do Sal in 1217, were conquered from the Moors by th
1 CONC e private initiative of noblemen. This does not mean that he was a weak or somehow cowardly man. The first years of his reign were marked instead by internal disturbances between Afonso and his brothers and sisters. The king manag
1 CONC ed to keep security within Portuguese borders only by outlawing and exiling his kin.
1 CONT
1 CONT Since military issues were not a government priority, Afonso established the state's administration and centralized power on himself. He designed the first set of Portuguese written laws. These were mainly concerned with private p
1 CONC roperty, civil justice, and minting. Afonso also sent ambassadors to European kingdoms outside the Iberian Peninsula and began amiable commercial relations with most of them.
1 CONT
1 CONT Other reforms included the always delicate matters with the pope. In order to get the independence of Portugal recognized by Rome, his grandfather, Afonso I, had to legislate an enormous amount of privileges to the Church. These e
1 CONC ventually created a state within the state. With Portugal's position as a country firmly established, Afonso II endeavoured to weaken the power of the clergy and to apply a portion of the enormous revenues of the Roman Catholic ch
1 CONC urch to purposes of national utility. These actions led to a serious diplomatic conflict between the pope and Portugal. After being excommunicated for his audacities by Pope Honorius III, Afonso II promised to make amends to the c
1 CONC hurch, but he died in 1223 before making any serious attempts to do so.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Marriage and descendants
1 CONT
1 CONT Afonso married Infanta Urraca of Castile, daughter of Alfonso VIII, King of Castile, and Leonora of Aquitaine, in 1208.
1 CONT Name Birth Death Notes
1 CONT By Urraca of Castile (1186-1220; married in 1208)
1 CONT Infante Sancho September 8, 1207 January 4, 1248 Succeeded him as Sancho II, 4th King of Portugal.
1 CONT Infante Afonso May 5, 1210 February 16, 1279 Succeeded his brother Sancho as Afonso III, 5th King of Portugal.
1 CONT Infanta Leonor (Eleanor) 1211 1231 Married Prince Valdemar, son of Valdemar II of Denmark and Margaret of Bohemia, daughter of Ottokar I of Bohemia.
1 CONT Infante Fernando a. 1217 c. 1243 Lord of Serpa.
1 CONT Vicente 1219 1219  
1 CONT Natural offspring
1 CONT João Afonso ? 1234 Natural son.
1 CONT Pedro Afonso c. 1210 ? Natural son.
1 CONT
